      "latency_ms": 1940.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 449,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is a compound made of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. Its famous chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. This means every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter:\n*   **Liquid:** The water we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, which expands and floats on liquid water (a rare and crucial property for aquatic life).\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air and steam from boiling water.\n\nPure liquid water is transparent, odorless, and tasteless. \n\n### 3. The \"Universal Solvent\"\nWater is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of its chemical structure, water molecules have a slight positive charge on one side and a slight negative charge on the other. This allows it to attract and pull apart other molecules (like salt and sugar), which is how it transports vital nutrients and minerals through our bodies and through ecosystems.\n\n### 4. Earth and Life\n*   **On Earth:** Water covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water), as well as in lakes, rivers, glaciers, and underground aquifers. \n*   **In Living Things:** Water is the fundamental building block of life. The human body is made up of about **60% water**. It regulates our body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and acts as the environment in which all cellular processes take place. Without water, life as we know it cannot survive."
